  "All five brands are issued by the same insurer, AAI Limited (Suncorp Group, AFSL 230859), and share claims operations. Shannons was skipped as instructed.",
  "Every current motor PDS in the group dates from late 2020 (AAMI 18 Sep 2020, Suncorp/GIO 25 Nov 2020, Apia 3 Nov 2020, Bingle 18 Dec 2020), amended by 2021 market-value and 2022 paint-protection SPDSs. None of these documents mention EVs, high-voltage batteries, charging cables or wall chargers.",
  "EV marketing: I couldn't find any AAMI or Suncorp Australia EV product or EV page. The Suncorp-group 'EV insurance' marketing found is from AMI in New Zealand (ami.co.nz: charger/cable covered, EV out-of-charge roadside). It is easy to confuse with AAMI and doesn't apply in NSW. AAMI's Australian roadside T&Cs have no out-of-charge service. A National Cover blog claims AAMI covers charging cables up to $1,500, but no AAMI document supports it.",
  "2026 discount purge across the group: Apia removed multi-policy and Drive Less Pay Less (19 Jan 2026) and NCB (1 Aug 2026). GIO removed multi-policy and Claims Free Savings (1 Aug 2026). Suncorp removed multi-policy, Claims Free Driver Rewards and NCB (3 Oct 2026). AAMI's 3 Oct 2026 changes list only motorcycle and caravan, so car Safe Driver Rewards appears to survive, but I couldn't get AAMI's 2026 car AIG. Comparison-site write-ups that still mention these discounts (e.g. Finder citing Apia's 65% NCB and Drive Less Pay Less) are stale.",
  "excessRange meaning: for AAMI, GIO, Suncorp and Apia the range is an optional voluntary excess added on top of a standard excess that varies by car and location and isn't published. PDS worked examples use a $650 (AAMI) or $600 (Suncorp, GIO, Apia) standard excess. For Bingle the range is the chosen standard excess itself ($700-$2,300 per the Dec 2023 guide).",
  "valueBasis: CHOICE (Aug 2026) says GIO Platinum and Suncorp Comprehensive Advantages are agreed value only, while GIO Comprehensive and the other two Suncorp tiers offer agreed or market value. The Apia PDS uses 'amount covered' (agreed), but CHOICE lists Apia as agreed or market. Bingle is market value only.",
  "Choice of repairer splits the group: GIO and Suncorp let you pick your own repairer; AAMI, Apia and Bingle don't. For an EX5 owner who wants a Geely-approved or EV-trained body shop, that matters.",
  "Market price context, not brand-specific: CHOICE Jan 2026 quotes put the average EV at $2,545 vs $1,702 for petrol, and Geely is the cheapest EV brand to insure at $1,622 for a new car (https://www.choice.com.au/money/insurance/car/articles/the-cost-of-insuring-electric-vehicles). Compare the Market's average BEV comprehensive quote was ~$2,300 in Mar 2026. Canstar's average NSW comprehensive premium is $2,792 (Oct 2026). Youi publishes a $1,082-$1,978 EX5 range (https://www.youi.com.au/car-insurance/insurance-by-make/geely).",
  "No Geely EX5-specific evidence (acceptance, vehicle-selector listing or quotes) was found for any Suncorp brand. Quote engines weren't run.",
